# Demo

The **manifest** — the file the weave reads first to learn what this workbook is,
what it depends on, and how to ship. It's the same literate text as every other
file: a manifest is **not JSON**.

A workbook declares what it is with a plain struct — types inferred from defaults:

defmodule Workbook do
  defstruct name: "", tagline: "", facets: [], entry: nil, exports: [], version: "0.0.0"
end

workbook = %Workbook{
  name: "Demo",
  tagline: "A lead workbook: ingest, enrich, score, rank, draft, ship.",
  facets: [:app, :agent, :kit],
  entry: "02-pages/page.work",
  exports: [FinModel]
}

`:entry` is the leaf the app launches (the [[Sales Dashboard]]). `:exports` is the
public surface — only `FinModel` crosses the boundary, so a workbook that imports
this one sees the kit and nothing else.

## Dependencies

Addressed by name, not a URL in a lockfile. A workbook can even depend on itself —
`:finmodel` is the kit this tree exports.

deps = [
  {:finmodel, "~> 0.4", path: "kits/kit.work"},
  {:work_ui,  "~> 1.0", kit: "work-*"},
  {:svelte,   "~> 5.0", island: :client},
  {:solid,    "~> 1.8", island: :client}
]

Foreign compute units ([[Forecast]] Rust+Zig, [[Risk]] Python) aren't packages —
their source is inline, compiled to wasm at weave time.

## The weave checks

The gate that runs before a bundle ships — a red check ships nothing.

checks = [:resolve_refs, :audit_caps, :run_tests]

Next: [[Welcome]] if you skipped it, or [[Types]] to start building.
